L000455	\section{Two-dimensional stochastic projected Gross-Pitaevskii equation}
L000456	The stochastic projected Gross-Pitaevskii equation (SPGPE) is a description based on $c$-field methods \cite{blakie2008dynamics}, consisting of dividing the system's modes into a coherent region ($\mathcal{C}$), which includes the low-lying, highly populated levels, and an incoherent region of high-energy, sparsely occupied states that act as a thermal reservoir. The evolution of $\mathcal{C}$ is captured by a classical field $\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}$, which evolves according to
L000457	\begin{equation}\label{2D_dimensionless_SPGPE}
L000458	    d{\Psi_\mathcal{C}} = \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{C}} \left[ -(i + \gamma) \left( H_{\textrm{sp}} + g |\Psi_\mathcal{C}|^{2} -\mu(t) \right) \Psi_\mathcal{C} \, dt + d \eta \right], 
L000459	\end{equation}
L000460	where length, time, and temperature are measured in units of $l \coloneqq \sqrt{\hbar^2 / \left( m E_{\textrm{sc}} \right)}$, $\hbar / E_{\textrm{sc}}$, and $E_{\textrm{sc}} / k_B$, respectively. Here $E_{\textrm{sc}}$ sets the energy scale, $m$ is the mass of a boson, $\hbar$ the reduced Planck constant, $k_B$ the Boltzmann constant, $\mu$ the chemical potential, $\gamma$ the dissipation rate, 
L000461	$
L000462	g 
L000463	= 
L000464	2 \sqrt{2 \pi} a_s / l_{\perp} 
L000465	$ 
L000466	the coupling strength of the repulsive delta-function interaction between bosons 
L000467	in a quasi-two-dimensional system \cite{pitaevskii2003bose,Bradley2015}, with 
L000468	$a_s$ the s-wave scattering length and 
L000469	$l_{\perp}=\sqrt{\hbar/(mw_{\perp})}$ the harmonic-oscillator length associated with the transverse confinement. 
L000470	From now on, we will use our scaled units throughout the supplementary material. 
L000471	$H_{\textrm{sp}}=-(1/2)\nabla^{2}+V(\boldsymbol{r})$ denotes the single-particle Hamiltonian, and its eigenfunctions $\phi_{n}(\boldsymbol{r})$ serve as a convenient basis to describe the $\mathcal{C}$ region. 
L000472	The noise term $d\eta$ is a complex Wiener process, with an autocorrelation function proportional to the temperature $T$. Specifically, it satisfies
L000473	\begin{equation}
L000474	\langle d \eta (\boldsymbol{r}, t) \rangle = 0, \quad \langle d \eta (\boldsymbol{r}, t) d \eta^{*}(\boldsymbol{r}', t) \rangle = 2T\gamma\, \delta_{\mathcal{C}}(\boldsymbol{r} - \boldsymbol{r}')\, dt,
L000475	\end{equation}
L000476	where $\delta_{\mathcal{C}}(\boldsymbol{r}-\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime})=\sum_{n\in \mathcal{C}}\phi_{n}(\boldsymbol{r})\phi^{*}_{n}(\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime})$
L000477	is the delta function restricted to the low-energy subspace. This subspace is spanned by the set of eigenfunctions $\{\phi_{n}\}_{n \in \mathcal{C}}$ with energies $\epsilon_{n}$ below a properly chosen cut-off $\epsilon_{cut}$. 
L000478	Finally, $\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{C}}$ denotes the projector operator,
L000479	which restricts the dynamics to the low-energy subspace.
L000480	Specifically,
L000481	$\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{C}}$ acts on a generic field $f(\boldsymbol{r})$ by projecting it onto the $\mathcal{C}$ region \cite{blakie2008dynamics}:
L000482	\begin{equation}\label{action_Projector_C}
L000483	    \mathcal{P}_\mathcal{C} f(\boldsymbol{r}) = \sum_{n:\epsilon_{n}<\epsilon_{\text{cut}}} \phi_{n}(\boldsymbol{r}) \int f(\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime}) \phi^{*}_{n}(\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime}) \, d^{2}\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime}.
L000484	\end{equation} 
L000485	The action of the projector is thus naturally implemented in the eigenbasis of the single-particle Hamiltonian $H_{\textrm{sp}}$, by setting a maximum eigenmode and ensuring that the dynamics does not develop components above this cutoff. The field $\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}$ is then conveniently represented as a sum over the corresponding basis states
L000486	\begin{equation}\label{decompose_Psi_C_eq}
L000487	    \Psi_{\mathcal{C}}(\boldsymbol{r},t)=\sum_{n\in \mathcal{C}} c_{n}(t)\phi_{n}(\boldsymbol{r}),
L000488	\end{equation}
L000489	with the weights $c_{n}$ encoding the particle occupation number in the $n$-th mode. Substituting this expansion into (\ref{2D_dimensionless_SPGPE}) yields the evolution equations for the coefficients $c_{n}$.
L000490	To integrate these equations numerically, we rely on the Julia library \texttt{DifferentialEquations.jl} \cite{rackauckas2017differentialequations}, employing a stability-optimized stochastic Runge-Kutta algorithm (SOSRA) for time propagation \cite{rackauckas2017adaptive,rackauckas2020stability}.
L000491	The simulations are performed in a periodic $L \times L$ homogeneous box (i.e., $V(\boldsymbol{r})=0$), with $\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}=0$ as the initial condition. The system is then relaxed for $t_{r}$ time units before beginning the quench.
L000492	Unless otherwise specified, we use the parameter values in Table \ref{tab:tab_param_vals}, motivated by the choices in \cite{Thudiyangal24}.
