L000605	\end{figure}
L000606	The extent of this range usually spans about one decade in $k$ or less, depending on the quench rate, reflecting the limited separation of characteristic length scales in atomic BECs.
L000607	In particular, the ratio of the system size $L$ to the healing length $\xi_h$ is typically $L/\xi_h \sim O(70)$ in experiments \cite{Chomaz15}, while in our simulation it is $L/\xi_h = 30\sqrt{2\mu_f} = 189.737$.
L000608	To mitigate this limitation and obtain a broader scaling regime, we resort to the principle of extended self-similarity of the velocity structure functions.
L000609	In particular, we consider the $p$-th order longitudinal velocity structure function $S_p \left( r \right)$, defined as 
L000610	\begin{equation}
L000611	S_{p}(r) = \Big\langle \big\vert[\boldsymbol{u}_{i}(\boldsymbol{R}+\boldsymbol{r}) - \boldsymbol{u}_{i}(\boldsymbol{R})]\cdot 
L000612	\boldsymbol{e}_r 
L000613	\big\vert^{p} \Big\rangle,
L000614	\end{equation}
L000615	where $\boldsymbol{u}_{i}$ denotes the incompressible component of the weighted velocity introduced in the main text, and 
L000616	$\boldsymbol{e}_r$ 
L000617	is the unit vector along $\boldsymbol{r}$. The brackets $\langle \cdot \rangle$ indicate averaging over all possible system positions $\boldsymbol{R}$ and orientations of 
L000618	$\boldsymbol{e}_r$. 
L000619	In the IR, Kolmogorov's 1941 (K41) theory predicts the power-law
L000620	\(
L000621	S_p(r) \propto r^{
L000622	\zeta_0 \left( p \right)
L000623	}
L000624	\), with $
L000625	\zeta_0 \left( p \right)
L000626	=p/3
L000627	$. For \(p = 2\), this scaling relation yields the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum \(E_i(k) \propto k^{-5/3}\) \cite{Kolmogorov41,Benzi1993,Dubrulle1994,Frisch1995}.
L000628	In Fig. \ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} (a), we show the structure functions $S_{p}(r)$ up to order $p=5$. The Kolmogorov power-law regime appears narrow, making it difficult to reliably extract the scaling exponents. To overcome this, we additionally plot $S_{p}$ as a function of $S_{3}$ in Fig. \ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} (c)-(f). Since $S_{3}\propto r$ according to K41, this representation preserves the same scaling exponents as $S_{p}(r)$.
L000629	Moreover, the scaling law extends beyond the IR, revealing the self-similar character of the velocity structure functions, known as extended self-similarity (ESS) \cite{Benzi1993}. 
L000630	In particular, Fig.~\ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} demonstrates that the relation $S_p(r) \propto [S_3(r)]^{\zeta(p)}$ holds well beyond the inertial range for $\tau_Q = 350$, namely over $0.03 \lesssim r \lesssim l_v = 4.295$, while the corresponding inertial range lies within $d_v = 0.632 \lesssim r \lesssim l_v$ (see Fig.~2(b) in the main text). The fitted scaling relations in Figs.~\ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} (c - f) are given by
L000631	$S_1(r) = \left( 0.420 \pm 0.011 \right) \left\lbrack S_3(r) \right\rbrack^{0.439 \pm 0.011}$, 
L000632	$S_2(r) = \left( 0.457 \pm 0.021 \right) \left\lbrack S_3(r) \right\rbrack^{0.820 \pm 0.022}$, 
L000633	$S_4(r) = \left( 3.316 \pm 0.220 \right) \left\lbrack S_3(r) \right\rbrack^{1.069 \pm 0.033}$, 
L000634	and
L000635	$S_5(r) = \left( 13.794 \pm 1.061 \right) \left\lbrack S_3(r) \right\rbrack^{1.118 \pm 0.038}$, 
L000636	obtained from the orthogonal distance regression 
L000637	using the whole range of $r$ in Fig. \ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} (a), 
L000638	with the uncertainties in $S_p(r)$ given by their corresponding standard deviations. 
L000639	This broader scaling range allows for a more reliable determination of the Kolmogorov exponents, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} (b). We observe deviations from the K41 prediction, $\zeta(p) = p/3$, 
L000640	consistent with results reported in both classical and quantum turbulence \cite{Benzi1993,Krstulovic2016,Zhao25}. Such deviations can be accounted for by a refinement of the K41 prediction. In particular, we test the K62 formulation, which introduces intermittency corrections and predicts $\zeta(p) = p / 3 - \kappa p (p - 3) / 2$,
L000641	where $\kappa$ is a constant \cite{Kolmogorov_1962}.
L000642	As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:ESS_Tq_350} (b), the measured exponents are in good agreement with the K62 prediction, with fitting parameter $\kappa = 0.114 \pm 0.006$. 
